📡 How to Calibrate and Update Your Raspberry Pi 5 Without Internet
Yes – you can fully calibrate and update your robot on-site without internet using SSH over a local network.

🔌 Step 1: Connect to Your Raspberry Pi 5
You need to connect your laptop to the Pi using one of these methods:

Option A: Ethernet Cable (Recommended – Most Stable)
Plug an Ethernet cable directly between your laptop and the Raspberry Pi 5.

The Pi should automatically get a 169.254.x.x IP address.

If not, you may need to set a static IP on the Pi (you can pre‑configure this before competition).

Option B: USB‑C Cable (If Ethernet Port Is Used)
The Pi can be configured for SSH over USB‑C.

This also powers the Pi, so you only need one cable.

Pre‑configure this before competition – it's a one‑time setup.

Option C: Wi‑Fi (If Available)
Connect both devices to the same Wi‑Fi network (if venue provides one).

Find the Pi's IP address.

💻 Step 2: SSH into the Pi
Once connected, open a terminal on your laptop and run:

bash
ssh <username>@<pi-ip-address>
Example:

bash
ssh pi@169.254.1.1
Or if using USB‑C:

bash
ssh pi@raspberrypi.local
Enter your password when prompted.

📝 Step 3: Edit YAML Files (Calibration)
Once logged in, navigate to your project folder:

bash
cd /home/pi/wro_future_engineers_2026/config
Open the YAML file with nano:

bash
nano system_prompt_matrix.yaml
Use arrow keys to navigate.

Edit values as needed.

Save: Ctrl + O → press Enter.

Exit: Ctrl + X.

🔧 Step 4: Edit Python Code (If Needed)
If you need to update state_machine.py or other files:

bash
cd /home/pi/wro_future_engineers_2026/src
nano state_machine.py
Make your changes, save, and exit.

🚀 Step 5: Restart the Robot Service
After saving your changes, restart the robot service:

bash
sudo systemctl restart wro_robot.service
Check status:

bash
sudo systemctl status wro_robot.service
View live logs:

bash
sudo journalctl -u wro_robot.service -f
📂 Option: Transfer Files via USB Stick
If you have updated files on your laptop (e.g., new main.cpp or state_machine.py), you can transfer them via USB:

Copy files to a USB stick.

Plug the USB stick into the Raspberry Pi.

Mount and copy:

bash
sudo mount /dev/sda1 /mnt
cp /mnt/new_file.py /home/pi/wro_future_engineers_2026/src/
sudo umount /mnt
